 gto.com/events/2026/06/03/original_image9443fa54-015f-4eda-8ecd-1ae78d600a
 a2.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Prepare to move.\nToronto-based clean|breaks fuses tight groov
 es\, infectious funk\, and rock-fueled energy into a vibrant instrumental 
 sound. Guitarist Neil Hendry\, drummer Tim Shia\, and bassist Jordan Safer
  bring decades of stage experience from prestigious venues and festivals a
 cross Canada and beyond—with their recordings featured on radio airwaves
  around the world. Drawing on the locked-in legacy of The Meters\, the urg
 ency of The Fearless Flyers/Vulfpeck\, and classic influences like Al Gree
 n\, expect dance-inducing vibes all night long.\nTight grooves. Sharp brea
 ks. Instrumental funk-rock.\nEach month\, the band will be joined by a spe
 cial guest vocalist in their second set.\nSat June 27 – SHEZELLE\nAn in-
 demand vocalist whose credits include performances and recordings with Ark
 ells\, collaborating with Elise LeGrow\, and appearances alongside Chantal
  Kreviazuk\, Keshia Chanté\, Kreesha Turner\, Anjulie\, Tyler Shaw\, and 
 Digging Roots.\nSat July 25 – CHLOE WATKINSON\nA powerhouse soul-rock vo
 calist who has performed at Roy Thomson Hall\, Massey Hall\, Dundas Square
 \, and festivals across Canada and in the US and Europe\, sharing stages w
 ith artists including Kim Mitchell and Jill Barber.\nSat Aug 29 – Guest 
 vocalist to be announced. Follow @cleanbreaksband on Instagram for updates
 .
LOCATION:Emmet Ray
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/clean-breaks-monthly-residency-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Conversation: Artist Natalia Romik and Curator Kuba Szreder with M
 atthew Jocelyn
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260627T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260627T150000
UID:blogto-236363@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/06/10/original_imagec9823ef9-5707-407d-9207-65ada0dd64
 62.jpg
DESCRIPTION:The conversation features artist Natalia Romik\, curator Kuba 
 Szreder\, and Koffler Arts' General Director Matthew Jocelyn discussing th
 e exhibition Plague Crystals. This series of crystal towers is created fro
 m repurposed vessels containing objects that symbolize a broad range of co
 ntemporary plagues. Romik's conception of "plague" extends beyond infectio
 us disease to include political\, economic\, religious\, social\, and envi
 ronmental issues. While the artist selected many of the symbolic objects h
 erself\, she solicited nominations for others from academics and community
  members. The discussion explores the artistic process\, the symbolic obje
 cts\, and how society grapples with dire straits.\nDr. Natalia Romik is a 
 public historian\, architect\, and artist. Her work focuses on Jewish memo
 ry and Holocaust commemoration in Eastern Europe\, particularly Poland and
  Ukraine. She has collaborated as a curator and exhibition designer at POL
 IN Museum of the History of Polish Jews. She is a winner of the 2022 Dan D
 avid Prize\, the largest prize in the field of history in the world.\nKuba
  Szreder PhD is a researcher\, curator\, and lecturer at the Academy of Fi
 ne Arts in Warsaw. He is the editor and author of several catalogues\, boo
 ks\, readers\, book chapters\, articles\, and manifestos that scrutinize t
 he social\, economic\, and theoretical aspects of the expanded field of ar
 t. In 2025\, together with Kacper Greń and the Institute of Network Cultu
 res in Amsterdam\, he published the visual essay Duckrabbits Unveiled: A S
 neak Peek at Postartistic Theory and Practice.
LOCATION:Koffler Arts
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/conversation-artist-natalia-romik-and-cu
 rator-kuba-szreder-with-matthew-jocelyn-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
