BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:To Catch a Thief - 4K Restoration
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260628T153000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260628T173000
UID:blogto-235046@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/05/14/original_image1def60f0-b0e3-456a-b6f4-80782b14b4
 5d.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Hitchcock's superbly insouciant crime caper from 1955 must sur
 ely be one of the last movies in which the American super-rich are indulge
 d so extravagantly and adoringly–the kind of people who stub their cigar
 ettes out in fried eggs. The south of France is resplendent in all its cyn
 icism and discretion. Diamonds are what the movie worships amid the sapphi
 re-blue of the Mediterranean and cloudless skies\; Hitchcock wittily begin
 s by disrupting those tourist images with a scream of horror from a woman 
 whose valuables have been swiped. Cary Grant is John Robie\, the reformed 
 cat burglar living quietly on the Côte d'Azur\, under suspicion for carry
 ing out a spate of daring jewel thefts in Nice and Cannes. He can only cle
 ar his name by collaring the real culprit–and while on this person's tra
 il\, he encounters the beautiful heiress Francie: the stunningly ice-blond
  Grace Kelly\, pampered\, bored and turned on by John's reputation. Her ow
 n jewels are glittering symbols of sexual unavailability\, and there is so
 mething almost outrageously metaphorical in their verbal fencing in her su
 ite at the Carlton hotel\, as the fireworks explode outside. Francie finds
  something inauthentic in Robie: "like an American in an English movie". W
 ell\, yes\, perhaps. But Grant's debonair and oddly unlocatable mid-Atlant
 ic identity is absolutely right for the part. Peter Bradshaw\, The Guardia
 n\nHitchcock's superbly insouciant crime caper from 1955 must surely be on
 e of the last movies in which the American super-rich are indulged so extr
 avagantly and adoringly–the kind of people who stub their cigarettes out
  in fried eggs. The south of France is resplendent in all its cynicism and
  discretion. Diamonds are what the movie worships amid the sapphire-blue o
 f the Mediterranean and cloudless skies\; Hitchcock wittily begins by disr
 upting those tourist images with a scream of horror from a woman whose val
 uables have been swiped. Cary Grant is John Robie\, the reformed cat burgl
 ar living quietly on the Côte d'Azur\, under suspicion for carrying out a
  spate of daring jewel thefts in Nice and Cannes. He can only clear his na
 me by collaring the real culprit–and while on this person's trail\, he e
 ncounters the beautiful heiress Francie: the stunningly ice-blond Grace Ke
 lly\, pampered\, bored and turned on by John's reputation. Her own jewels 
 are glittering symbols of sexual unavailability\, and there is something a
 lmost outrageously metaphorical in their verbal fencing in her suite at th
 e Carlton hotel\, as the fireworks explode outside. Francie finds somethin
 g inauthentic in Robie: "like an American in an English movie". Well\, yes
 \, perhaps. But Grant's debonair and oddly unlocatable mid-Atlantic identi
 ty is absolutely right for the part. Peter Bradshaw\, The Guardian\nIn Eng
 lish and French\, with English subtitles.\nIn English and French\, with En
 glish subtitles.
LOCATION:Hot Docs Cinema
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/to-catch-a-thief-4k-restoration-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
